verdict.fix:
1. Restrict pruning so the replacement argument actually holds. In formThread (Typer.swift ~3721), record more than the ancestor type: also record a snapshot of the assumed-given identifiers in the environment at that point, i.e. store `ancestry[id] = ancestors.appending((queried, Set(environment.givens.ids)))`. In summon (lines 3642-3650), prune only when both of these hold:
   - the reified types are equal;
   - the subgoal's current assumed givens are a subset of the ancestor's snapshot (no extra sibling premises are available to the subgoal).
   Then the subgoal's derivation can be no cheaper, in layers or in group-offset penalties, than one available at the ancestor's position. This case (the subgoal sees extra sibling premises) is exactly the one that breaks both LC-1 and LC-2.
2. As a safety net, if a summon under loopcheck returns no result, or bumps summon.depthExhausted, re-run that query without loopcheck. Failures then can never be introduced; the cost is paid only on queries that fail anyway.
3. Add lc04 and lc05 as regression tests.
4. Correct REPORT §5.5:
   - drop "smaller term that completes in an earlier BFS layer" as a general claim;
   - state that layer depth and penalties depend on the assumed sibling premises;
   - say diagnostics were identical only on the test corpus;
   - cite lc04 as a counterexample.

